If it's a melee-only attack (similar to curse item spell, destroy armor
spell, eel wrapping), it makes Elbereth more important/powerful.
And makes athames more useful.

Consistency would require bags of holding to be unaffected, only
non-magical containers (compare with what happens when throwing it).
This seems very undesirable, just making BoH-less ascensions even harder
than they already are.

Another downside is that important wand types (like teleportation, fire,
death) being breakable is a big disadvantage.

> Another idea would be to place a curse on players,
> which would be more annoying than threatening; over a
> random period of time, you have a chance each turn
> of losing one to four stat points, get sick (like
> with tripes or rotten eggs), confused or stunned.

> The disenchanter idea could be carried further as
> well; some monsters could steal charges from wands
> in the inventory and maybe even use them against you.

Angband has this; the monster is healed considerably, proportional to
the number of charges stolen. Consequently, meleeing such a monster
lasts at least until all wands and staffs have been discharged.
Therefore, this means "no wands no staffs" in the endgame, where many
monsters have such an attack. Fortunately, Angband has items similar to
wands, called rods, which are not affected; they have a timeout instead
of a number of charges.

NetHack has bags which would probably block such attacks, so it would
not make wands completely useless as in Angband. It removes some things
which "always work" and I hate that. What should I do now to take care
of situations where the wiz summons two master mind flayers on opposite
sides of me?

